The handlers `-fsanitize=undefined` calls (`__ubsan_handle_add_overflow` and the rest, each
with its `_abort` form), so that a UBSan build needs no runtime: libc programs and freestanding
ones alike link them from libca.a. A failed check is a ca trap: a context taking it fails with
the check as its reason (`signed integer overflow`, `shift out of bounds`, `misaligned pointer
use`, ...) at the source line that failed, and the program goes on; with none, the program
writes one line and stops by an illegal instruction:

    ca: undefined behavior: signed integer overflow at tests/ubsan_trap.cpp:13:15

The handlers are weak, so a sanitizer runtime linked in (ASan's carries UBSan's) keeps its own.
`make SAN=undefined` builds the library and the tests so, with `-fno-sanitize-recover`; no
check returns. tests/ubsan_context.cpp shows a context taking the checks, tests/ubsan_trap.cpp
the line printed without one.
